  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{\u2032}-3.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{\u2032}-3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+p-3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =(xp+p-3)^{\u2032} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{\u2032}+{p}^{\u2032} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{\u2032}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+1=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+p-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+p-3 \\\\  & =(-1)p+p-3 \\\\  & =-p+p-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-3.\n$$",
